{"probe_id":"math.proof_construction.L2.005","canonical_id":"math.proof_construction","category":"math","level":"L2","axis_values":{"abstraction_level":"concrete","proof_technique":"direct"},"prompt":"Let p, q, r, and s be integers with q, s ≠ 0. To prove that p/q + r/s = (ps + rq)/(qs) is rational, what kind of numbers are both ps + rq and qs? (The nonzero condition for qs follows from q, s ≠ 0.) Output a single common noun (plural).","ground_truth":"integers","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"integers","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_substring":false,"allow_terminal_punctuation":true},"tags":["math","proof"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"math.proof_construction.L3.001","canonical_id":"math.proof_construction","category":"math","level":"L3","axis_values":{"abstraction_level":"abstract_algebra","proof_technique":"contradiction_or_induction"},"prompt":"Prove that there are infinitely many prime numbers. Use a proof by contradiction. Your proof must (a) explicitly assume there are only finitely many primes, (b) construct a number from the assumed list, and (c) derive a contradiction. Write your proof in 4–8 sentences.","ground_truth":"Suppose for contradiction that there are only finitely many primes p_1, p_2, ..., p_k. Consider N = p_1·p_2·...·p_k + 1. N is not divisible by any p_i (since it leaves remainder 1). So N has a prime factor not in our list, contradicting the assumption.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[{\"id\": \"finite_assumption\", \"description\": \"明确以反证法假设所有素数可列为有限列表，例如 p_1,...,p_k。\"}, {\"id\": \"construction\", \"description\": \"从该列表构造 N=p_1p_2...p_k+1，或数学上等价的乘积加一构造。\"}, {\"id\": \"nondivisibility\", \"description\": \"说明每个列表中的 p_i 都不能整除 N，例如 N 除以 p_i 余 1。\"}, {\"id\": \"contradiction\", \"description\": \"说明 N 本身为素数或 N 有某个不在列表中的素因子，并明确指出这与有限列表假设矛盾。\"}, {\"id\": \"format\", \"description\": \"回答为 4 至 8 个英文句子。\"}]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["math","proof","euclid"],"notes":"substring_all: requires the words 'finite', 'contradict', 'prime', and the construction '+ 1'"}
{"probe_id":"math.proof_construction.L3.002","canonical_id":"math.proof_construction","category":"math","level":"L3","axis_values":{"abstraction_level":"concrete","proof_technique":"contradiction_or_induction"},"prompt":"Prove that √2 is irrational using a proof by contradiction. Suppose, for contradiction, that √2 = p/q, where p and q are integers, q ≠ 0, and gcd(p,q)=1. Your proof must derive that p is even, then that q is even, and explain why this contradicts gcd(p,q)=1. Write your proof in 4–8 sentences.","ground_truth":"Suppose √2 = p/q in lowest terms, with p,q integers and gcd(p,q)=1. Then 2q² = p², so p² is even, so p is even. Write p = 2m. Then 2q² = 4m², so q² = 2m², so q is also even. But then gcd(p,q) ≥ 2, contradicting gcd(p,q) = 1.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"Uses a contradiction assumption that √2 is represented as p/q with integers p,q, q≠0, in lowest terms, or equivalently gcd(p,q)=1.\", \"Squares or otherwise correctly derives p²=2q² (equivalently 2q²=p²).\", \"Correctly concludes that p is even from p² being even.\", \"Correctly uses p=2m, or an equivalent argument, to conclude that q is even.\", \"States that p and q therefore share factor 2 and that this contradicts lowest terms/coprimality.\", \"Uses a proof-by-contradiction structure.\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["math","proof","irrational"],"notes":"Note: 'contradic' substring matches both 'contradiction' and 'contradicts'"}
{"probe_id":"math.proof_construction.L3.003","canonical_id":"math.proof_construction","category":"math","level":"L3","axis_values":{"abstraction_level":"concrete","proof_technique":"contradiction_or_induction"},"prompt":"Prove by mathematical induction that the sum 1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2n − 1) equals n² for every positive integer n. Your proof must include (1) a base case for n = 1, (2) an inductive hypothesis, and (3) an inductive step. Write the full proof in 5–10 sentences.","ground_truth":"For n = 1, the left-hand side is 1, and 1 = 1², so the statement holds in the base case. Now assume that for some arbitrary positive integer k, 1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2k - 1) = k². This is the inductive hypothesis. For n = k + 1, the sum through the next odd number is 1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2k - 1) + (2k + 1). By the inductive hypothesis, this equals k² + 2k + 1. Since k² + 2k + 1 = (k + 1)², the formula holds for k + 1. Therefore, by mathematical induction, 1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2n - 1) = n² for every positive integer n.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Accept only if the response is a 5–10 sentence induction proof and correctly includes all of: (1) a base case n=1 establishing 1=1²; (2) an inductive hypothesis for an arbitrary positive integer k that the sum through 2k-1 equals k²; (3) an inductive step adding the next odd term 2k+1; (4) correct algebra k²+(2k+1)=(k+1)²; and (5) a conclusion that the identity holds for every positive integer n by induction. Reject proofs with an incorrect next term, invalid algebra, omitted required induction component, or an unsupported conclusion."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["math","proof","induction"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"math.proof_construction.L3.004","canonical_id":"math.proof_construction","category":"math","level":"L3","axis_values":{"abstraction_level":"abstract_algebra","proof_technique":"structural"},"prompt":"Prove by mathematical induction that, for every integer n ≥ 1, 5 divides 6^n − 1. Your proof must include the base case n = 1, an inductive hypothesis, an inductive step from k to k + 1, and a concluding sentence invoking mathematical induction. Write 5–10 sentences.","ground_truth":"For the base case n=1, we have 6^1−1=5, which is divisible by 5. Assume for some integer k≥1 that 5 divides 6^k−1. Then there is an integer m such that 6^k−1=5m, so 6^k=5m+1. Hence 6^(k+1)−1=6·6^k−1=6(5m+1)−1=30m+5=5(6m+1). Therefore 5 divides 6^(k+1)−1. Since the base case and inductive step hold, mathematical induction shows that 5 divides 6^n−1 for every integer n≥1.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"States or correctly verifies the base case n=1, obtaining 6^1−1=5 or an equivalent divisibility statement.\", \"Introduces a valid inductive hypothesis for an arbitrary k≥1 that 5 divides 6^k−1.\", \"Correctly derives that 5 divides 6^(k+1)−1, for example by substituting 6^k=5m+1 or by using 6≡1 (mod 5).\", \"Concludes that the statement holds for every integer n≥1 by mathematical induction.\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["math","proof","induction"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"math.proof_construction.L3.005","canonical_id":"math.proof_construction","category":"math","level":"L3","axis_values":{"abstraction_level":"abstract_algebra","proof_technique":"direct"},"prompt":"Prove that the empty set ∅ is a subset of every set X. Write a 1–3 sentence proof explaining that the inclusion holds because there are no elements of ∅ that could violate it. Then, on a separate final line, output the single word that completes: “the implication is _____ true.”","ground_truth":"For every x, if x ∈ ∅, then x ∈ X. Since no x belongs to ∅, the antecedent is always false, so the implication holds for every x and ∅ ⊆ X.\nvacuously","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Accept iff all conditions hold: (1) the proof is 1–3 sentences before the final line; (2) it correctly concludes that ∅ ⊆ X for arbitrary X; (3) it explains this by saying that ∅ has no elements, or equivalently that for every x the condition x ∈ ∅ is false, or that there can be no counterexample to inclusion; (4) it explicitly connects that fact to vacuous truth, either by naming the implication as vacuously true or by an equivalent accurate explanation; and (5) the final line consists solely of “vacuously”, case-insensitive. Do not require any exact implication syntax or particular wording."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["math","proof","sets","vacuous"],"notes":"Used judge_rubric here because deterministic substring scoring cannot reliably catch vacuous-truth proof structure"}
